                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "(not really a review)\nI played Waves (the original) a lot when I was a young teen. I was going through a particularly hard time in my life, my parents recently separated, my mom quickly remarried to a man in another state and we didn't get a long. I didn't have much friends as I struggled at home and found it hard to connect to people at school. A lot of the time when I was at home I would stay in my room and play games to take my mind off of the difficulties in life. Waves was particularly good at this as the chaotic nature and tense moments would force me to focus on what was in front, not around me. The music was tense and loud, perfect for drowning my mom and (then) step-dad screaming at each other through the walls.\n\nThe developer behind Waves and Waves 2 really understands how to grab the players attention and hold it. The flow of the gameplay is very dynamic and varies depending on how well your doing. Enemies, obstacles and even the music increase or decrease in intensity based on your performance. If you're doing very well and have a huge multiplier, expect hundreds of diverse enemies to come barreling at you at mack 10. If you keep getting hit the game will likely slow down for a moment, giving you time to breath and reset. I really love this attention to the player, it keeps you involved without holding your hand too much, it shows the developer really thought about how players of different skill levels may enjoy the game.\n\nFinding about the developer losing their battle with cancer really saddened me. I never got the opportunity to tell them how much their game made an impact on me and how it kept me sane during tough times. I hope there's an afterlife for people like this and hope that their family is well and comfortable, able to live happy fulfilling lives.",
